mid", "targetName": "Red Pyramid", "context": "The Red Pyramid is framed within the broader context of evolving Egyptian funerary practices."}], "places": [{"name": "Dahshur, Egypt", "role": "Archaeological site"}, {"name": "Giza, Egypt", "role": "Nearby pyramid complex"}, {"name": "Memphis, Egypt", "role": "Former capital"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Red Pyramid", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "red-pyramid"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q135332", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_Pyramid"], "tags": ["Egypt", "archaeological site", "Africa", "Architecture & Built Works", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Red Pyramid left a notable mark on Classical history, documented across 41 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "The Red Pyramid represents a significant advancement in pyramid construction that shaped the architectural landscape of ancient Egypt, influencing subsequent royal tombs and the cultural significance of pyramids for millennia.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-29T01:35:03Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#28343035518", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Red Pyramid is a archaeological site from Africa, dating to 2589 BCE. It holds notable significance in its field. Holds notable historical significance, documented across 41 language editions of Wikipedia. Relevant to cause-and-effect analysis.", "newValue": "The Red Pyramid, located near Dahshur in Egypt, dates back to approximately 2589 BCE and stands as one of the earliest true pyramids built during the Fourth Dynasty of the Old Kingdom. Commissioned by Pharaoh Sneferu, this monumental structure signifies a pivotal transformation in ancient Egyptian architectural practices and represents the culmination of earlier pyramid designs. Its unique red limestone, which gives it its name, and its distinct architectural style mark a departure from the step pyramids that preceded it, showcasing advances in engineering and aesthetics that would influence subsequent generations of builders.

Constructed using approximately 1.6 million blocks of limestone, the Red Pyramid is notable for its smooth, angled sides, which create a true pyramid shape rather than the step-like structures of earlier phases. The pyramid's base spans about 220 meters on each side and originally rose to a height of 104 meters, though erosion has reduced it. This structure served not only as a tomb for Sneferu but also as a demonstration of the centralized power of the pharaoh, the economic resources of the state, and the skilled labor available to the Egyptians during this period.

